/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A thin-walled section such as an angle, channel, Z or hat is a composite shape: a set of flat plate elements joined along their edges.
   - σel is the local buckling stress (N/mm²); k = 4.0 for an element with both long edges simply supported and k = 0.425 for one with a free edge; b the flat width of the element (mm); t its thickness (mm).
   - Choose k for the edge condition first.
   - Local buckling does not end the strength of the section.
   - Both methods fit tests on short lengths of formed and extruded sections.
   - σcs is the crippling stress of the angle (N/mm²); σcy the compressive yield stress (N/mm²); E Young's modulus (N/mm²); a and b the leg lengths (mm); t the thickness (mm); Ce = 0.316 when both leg edges are free, 0.342 when one is free and 0
   - A channel, Z or hat is cut into angles at the middle of its webs.
   - Gerard correlated the same kind of tests with a single parameter that counts how many flat elements the section has for its area.
   - A is the section area (mm²); g is the number of flanges plus the number of cuts needed to divide the section into angles; the other symbols as above.
   - For two-corner sections (Z, J and channel) Gerard gives a separate formula of the same kind with its own constants, taken from the data sheet used in the course.
*/
