/* book Worked example: Q: An extruded equal angle has legs of 30 mm and is 1.5 mm thick. The alloy has E = 72 GPa, ν = 0.33 and σcy = 300 MPa. Find the local buckling stress of one leg, and the crippling stress and load by Needham's and by Gerard's method.
   1. Area: A ≈ (30 + 30) × 1.5 = 90 mm².
   2. Local buckling of a leg (one free edge, k = 0.425): σel = 0.425 × π² × 72,000/(12 × 0.8911) × (1.5/30)² = 0.425 × 66,455 × 0.0025 = 70.6 MPa.
   3. Needham: b′ = (30 + 30)/2 = 30 mm, b′/t = 20; both leg edges are free, so Ce = 0.316. √(σcy E) = √(300 × 72,000) = 4,647.6 MPa.
   4. σcs = 0.316 × 4,647.6/200.75 = 1,468.6/9.457 = 155.3 MPa, so Pcs = 155.3 × 90 = 13,976 N.
   5. Gerard: an angle has two flanges and needs no cut, so g = 2. g t²/A = 2 × 2.25/90 = 0.050; √(E/σcy) = √240 = 15.49; product 0.7746.
   6. σcs = 0.56 × 0.77460.85 × 300 = 0.56 × 0.8048 × 300 = 135.2 MPa, so Pcs = 135.2 × 90 = 12,169 N.
   7. Checks: both are below σcy = 300 MPa, so no cap is needed, and both are about twice σel: the corner carries load long after the legs buckle. The methods differ by 15%; take the lower for design.
   Answer: σel = 70.6 MPa; Needham σcs = 155 MPa (Pcs = 14.0 kN); Gerard σcs = 135 MPa (Pcs = 12.2 kN). */
