/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An open section resists torque mainly through its tiny St Venant constant J = Σbt³/3, plus resistance to warping when its ends or neighbours stop the section warping freely.
   - Let x₀ and y₀ locate S from the centroid C.
   - When C and S coincide, as in an I-section or a cruciform, the coupling vanishes: the column buckles either by bending about one axis at the Euler load, or by pure twisting about its axis, the torsional instability that short cruciforms show
   - For a pin-ended column free to warp at its ends, three uncoupled loads are defined:
   - Px, Py are the Euler loads for bending about the centroidal x and y axes (N); Pt the torsional buckling load (N); Ix, Iy the centroidal second moments (mm⁴); A the area (mm²); G the shear modulus (N/mm²); J the torsion constant (mm⁴); Cw th
   - GJ is St Venant's resistance and π²ECw/L² the warping resistance; the second fades as L grows.
   - x₀, y₀ are the coordinates of the shear centre from the centroid (mm).
   - For a section symmetric about x (y₀ = 0) the cubic splits: P = Py is a pure Euler mode, and bending about x couples with twist through (Px − P)(Pt − P) − P²x₀²/r₀² = 0.
   - Euler's formula σ = π²E/(L/ρ)² holds for slender columns, with ρ = √(I/A) the radius of gyration.
   - σc is the column failing stress (N/mm²); σcs the crippling stress (N/mm²); L′ the effective length (mm), equal to L for pinned ends; ρ the least radius of gyration (mm).
*/
