/* book Worked example: Q: A pin-ended channel column 800 mm long has a web of 60 mm and two flanges of 30 mm (mid-line), all 2 mm thick, with E = 70 GPa and G = 27 GPa. The channel is symmetric about the horizontal x axis. Find the Euler loads, the torsional load and the flexural–torsional buckling load, and say which mode governs.
   1. Area and centroid: A = 2 × (60 + 2 × 30) = 240 mm²; x̄ = b²t/A = 7.5 mm from the web, towards the flanges.
   2. Shear centre: e = 3b²/(h + 6b) = 2700/240 = 11.25 mm outside the web, so x₀ = 7.5 + 11.25 = 18.75 mm from C, and y₀ = 0.
   3. Second moments: Ix = t h³/12 + 2bt(h/2)² = 36,000 + 108,000 = 144,000 mm⁴; Iy = h t x̄² + 2[t b³/12 + b t(b/2 − x̄)²] = 22,500 mm⁴.
   4. Torsion: J = t³(h + 2b)/3 = 320 mm⁴; Cw = t b³h²(3b + 2h)/[12(6b + h)] = 1.417 × 10⁷ mm⁶.
   5. r₀² = (144,000 + 22,500)/240 + 18.75² = 693.75 + 351.56 = 1045.3 mm².
   6. Euler loads: Px = π² × 70,000 × 144,000/800² = 155,446 N; Py = π² × 70,000 × 22,500/800² = 24,288 N.
   7. Torsional load: GJ = 8,640,000 N·mm², π²ECw/L² = 15,301,742 N·mm², Pt = (8,640,000 + 15,301,742)/1045.3 = 22,904 N.
   8. Coupled mode (x with twist): x₀²/r₀² = 0.3363. (Px − P)(Pt − P) − 0.3363P² = 0 becomes 0.6637P² − 178,350P + 3.560 × 10⁹ = 0, whose smaller root is P = 21,718 N. Substituting back leaves a residual of 1.1e-06 N², so the root is right.
   9. Comparison: Py = 24.3 kN, Pt = 22.9 kN, flexural–torsional 21.7 kN. The flexural–torsional mode governs, 11% below the lower Euler load.
   Answer: Px = 155.4 kN, Py = 24.3 kN, Pt = 22.9 kN; the column buckles by bending and twisting together at 21.7 kN. */
