/* book Worked example 2: Q: The formed channel of the crippling chapter (web 60 mm, flanges 25 mm, 1.5 mm thick, A = 165 mm², σcs = 179.4 MPa, E = 72 GPa) is used as a pin-ended column 500 mm long. Find its column failing stress by the Johnson parabola, using the least radius of gyration.
   1. Least second moment, about the axis parallel to the web (mid-line dimensions): Iy = 10,298 mm⁴ for the mid-line area 165 mm².
   2. ρ = √(10,298/165) = 7.90 mm, so L′/ρ = 500/7.90 = 63.3.
   3. Tangent point: √(2π²E/σcs) = √(2π² × 72,000/179.4) = 89.0. Since 63.3 < 89.0, the parabola applies.
   4. σc = 179.4 × [1 − 179.4 × 63.3²/(4π² × 72,000)] = 179.4 × 0.7472 = 134.0 MPa.
   5. Check: Euler alone would give π² × 72,000/63.3² = 177 MPa, almost the stub crippling stress. At this middling slenderness local buckling and bending interact, and the parabola gives the lower, realistic 134 MPa.
   6. Failing load: 134.0 × 165 = 22,118 N. A flexural–torsional check, as in the first example, should follow.
   Answer: L′/ρ = 63.3; σc = 134 MPa and the column fails at about 22.1 kN. */
